{{pp-extended|small=yes}}
{{AI-generated|date=November 2025}}
'''Dalit literature''' is a genre of Indian writing that focuses on the lives, experiences, and struggles of the [[Dalit]] community over centuries, in relation to the caste system and caste-based discrimination.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Joshi |first=Prakash Bal |date=2019-03-09 |title='Dalit Literatures in India' review: Voice of protest |language=en-IN |work=The Hindu |url=https://www.thehindu.com/books/books-reviews/dalit-literatures-in-india-review-voice-of-protest/article26471306.ece |access-date=2019-08-08 |issn=0971-751X}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Abedi |first=Razi |title=Dalit Literature: The Voice of the Downtrodden |url=https://gowanusbooks.com/dalit.htm |access-date=6 June 2025 |website=gowanusbooks.com}}</ref>  This literary genre encompasses various Indian languages such as [[Bhojpuri language|Bhojpuri]],<ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-08-20 |title=Bhojpuri में पढ़ीं दलित कविता के जन्मदाता: हमेशा याद रहे वाला रचनाकार हीरा |url=https://hindi.news18.com/news/bhojpuri-news/heera-dom-wrote-first-dalit-literature-know-how-the-article-was-published-in-saraswati-magazine-3702384.html |access-date=2025-03-10 |website=News18 हिंदी |language=hi}}</ref> [[Marathi language|Marathi]], [[Bengali language|Bangla]], [[Hindi]],<ref>Patterns of Dalit Literature  : Dr. N. Singh, Publisher  : Vani Prakashan, New Delhi-110002, Edition: 2012</ref> [[Kannada]], [[Punjabi language|Punjabi]],<ref name="Kumar2014">{{cite book |author=Akshaya Kumar |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=68ITAwAAQBAJ&pg=PP1 |title=Poetry, Politics and Culture: Essays on Indian Texts and Contexts |date=14 March 2014 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-317-80963-0 |pages=272–314}}</ref> [[Sindhi language|Sindhi]], [[Odia language|Odia]] and [[Tamil language|Tamil]] and includes narrative-styles like poems, short stories, and autobiographies. The movement started gaining influence during the mid-twentieth-century in independent India and has since spread across various Indian languages.<ref>{{cite news |date=March 19, 2002 |title=Dalit literature reflects oppression of Dalits |work=[[The Hindu]] |url=http://www.hindu.com/2009/09/09/stories/2009090958010200.htm |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090913083749/http://www.hindu.com/2009/09/09/stories/2009090958010200.htm |archive-date=September 13, 2009}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=February 26, 2008 |title=TAMIL: Dalit literature |work=[[The Hindu]] |url=http://www.hindu.com/br/2008/02/26/stories/2008022650051400.htm}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=August 6, 2006 |title=Healing with languages |work=[[The Hindu]] |url=http://www.hindu.com/lr/2006/08/06/stories/2006080600160400.htm |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060813174545/http://www.hindu.com/lr/2006/08/06/stories/2006080600160400.htm |archive-date=August 13, 2006}}</ref>

In the colonial and post-colonial period, [[Jyotirao Phule]]'s [[Gulamgiri]], published in 1873, became a seminal work describing the plight of the Untouchables in India. Authors such as [[Sharankumar Limbale]], [[Namdeo Dhasal]], and [[Bama (writer)|Bama]], and movements like the [[Dalit Panthers|Dalit Panther]] movement in [[Maharashtra]] as well as [[B. R. Ambedkar|Dr. B.R. Ambedkar]], a jurist, economist and social reformer, have played influential roles in shaping Dalit literature across India.

[[Dalit feminism|Dalit feminist writing]] such as the autobiographies and testimonios of Dalit women authors emphasizing the intersection of [[caste]], class, and gender in the context of [[social exclusion]] came through this movement. Dalit women authors, such as [[Urmila Pawar]] and [[Babytai Kamble|Baby Kamble]], have written extensively about the complexities of caste, class, and gender in Indian society.<ref>{{Citation |last=Zelliot |first=Eleanor |chapter=Dalit literature, language, and identity |date=2008-03-27 |title=Language in South Asia |pages=450–465 |editor-last=Kachru |editor-first=Braj B. |chapter-url=https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/CBO9780511619069A046/type/book_part |access-date=2024-10-31 |edition=1 |publisher=Cambridge University Press |doi=10.1017/cbo9780511619069.026 |isbn=978-0-521-78653-9 |editor2-last=Kachru |editor2-first=Yamuna |editor3-last=Sridhar |editor3-first=S. N.|chapter-url-access=subscription }}</ref>

