'''Soft chemistry''' (also known as '''chimie douce''') is a type of [[chemistry]] that uses reactions at ambient temperature in open reaction vessels with reactions similar to those occurring in biological systems.<ref name="Sanchez2010">{{Citation | doi =10.1016/j.crci.2009.06.001 | title ="Chimie douce": A land of opportunities for the designed construction of functional inorganic and hybrid organic-inorganic nanomaterials | year =2010 | last1 =Sanchez | first1 =C. | last2 =Rozes | first2 =L. | last3 =Ribot | first3 =F. | last4 =Laberty-Robert | first4 =C. | last5 =Grosso | first5 =D. | last6 =Sassoye | first6 =C. | last7 =Boissiere | first7 =C. | last8 =Nicole | first8 =L. | journal =Comptes Rendus Chimie | volume =13 | issue =1–2 | pages =3–39 | doi-access =free }}</ref><ref name="Gopalakrishnan1995">{{Citation | doi =10.1021/cm00055a001 | title =Chimie Douce Approaches to the Synthesis of Metastable Oxide Materials | year =1995 | last1 =Gopalakrishnan | first1 =J. | journal =Chemistry of Materials | volume =7 | pages =1265–1275 | issue =7 }}</ref>

